 "findings": [
  "Independents' votes fall away with distance about two and a half times as fast as Labor's or the Coalition's: 20 km from their best booth, a typical independent keeps about 54% of the share they get 1 km away, against about 79% for Labor and 78% for the Coalition (Greens: 57%).",
  "It is real, not an artefact of picking the best booth: the same measure around a randomly chosen booth gives almost nothing (median slope -0.04 against -0.26), and the major-party candidates, measured the same way, show the background clustering every candidate has.",
  "Regional independents fade faster than urban ones (about 53% left at 20 km against 66%), though urban seats are small and rarely stretch that far.",
  "A strong independent's vote spreads out as they become established: of 17 independents measured at more than one election, 12 had a flatter profile at their last run than their first (median change +0.09).",
  "Bigger votes are flatter (correlation 0.38 between share and slope): the 30%+ independents are the whole seat's candidate, the 10-15% ones are mostly a local phenomenon.",
  "Independents look like the Greens on this measure, but for a different reason: the Greens' vote clusters by who lives where, an independent's by where the candidate comes from. Untangling the two (demographics vs distance) is the next step, alongside the preference-flow work."
